{
  "dom_id": "step-q162544",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14095686/assets/efddbdddb824d26d081b43027c05ff5c2544fc183b0efdf555b17256dd9565a1.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*",
  "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D\u2286{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $\u2207\\cdot F=\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\u2282D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\u2282D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $\u03c8(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(2x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y)+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y-2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero.\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$",
